  "grammar_notes": [
    "Contains a suffix cognate, a modal suffix marking potential states",
    "Numbers for seven and eight are derived from the roots for two and three, respectively, but with an apparent reversal in Woccon",
    "In nearly all Siouan languages the numbers eleven and twelve mean ten plus one and ten plus two",
    "Winnop twenty is a compound of pi * wi ten and nup two",
    "Some number forms may represent subtractive formations meaning 'ten lacking two' or 'two away from ten'",
    "Object-verb syntax",
    "Use of reduplication to signal frequency or intensity with both verb roots and noun roots by repeating the entire root",
    "Third person singular subjects were unmarked in Woccon",
    "Third person plural prefix |i-| used in some cases",
    "Pronominal objects are marked by proclitics, except third person singular objects which are unmarked",
    "Independent modal suffix used in Woccon",
    "Participial mode exists in Woccon",
    "Woccon form ikettau is apparently a participle",
    "Wechere contains the independent modal suffix, and thus literally means 'there are nine'",
    "Woccon ends with the independent modal suffix |-re|",
    "Cuttaune may be ended by interrogative mode marker |-ne| and means 'That's all?'",
    "Second person singular prefix |ya-| used in possessed forms",
    "Third person singular affix |-h-| exists",
    "Independent mode marker |-re| exists",
    "Postpositions are used (e.g. 'mau' meaning 'there')"
  ],
  "pronunciation_notes": [
    "The first n in ynpyupseunne is almost certainly an inverted u",
    "Woccon shared the same inventory of vowels as the Catawba language",
    "Four short oral vowels (i,e,a,u), four long oral vowels (i-, e-,a-,u-) and four nasal vowels (į ę, ą ų)",
    "In most cases Woccon shows a long oral vowel",
    "Woccon words show nasalization of the r's to n's due to the medial nasal vowel"
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    "Documentation of words/spellings exists for Woccon language",
    "Woccon vocabulary was documented by John Lawson in 'A new voyage to Carolina' (1860), pages 225-230 & 234",
    "Lawson traveled through several nations of Indians and documented their customs and language",
    "The vocabulary includes terms for European trade goods like rum, blankets, gunpowder, and clothing items",
    "Animal skin terminology is extensive, reflecting importance in Woccon material culture",
    "Woccon had distinct terms for Englishman (winsohore) and Indians (yauh-he)",
    "Waccon towns were named Yupwauremau and Tooptatmeer",
    "Woccon language data comes from Adelung and Vater 1806 publication",
    "Richard T. Carter analyzed Woccon's genetic affiliations and historical significance in 1980",
    "There are errors in reprinting of Lawson's book in the 1967 version versus the 1709 version",
    "Higher numerals are believed to be confused in the Woccon vocabulary",
    "Lawson may have made errors by transposing numbers for seven and eight, and similar errors in recordings of ten, eleven and twelve",
    "The root [yup-] appears to be the root for wood",
    "Woccon is a language of North Carolina",
    "Woccon is a Siouan language",
    "Woccon words recorded by Lawson provide historical documentation",
    "Woccon has certain Catawba cognates that help reconstitute the phoneme inventory",
    "The pea word for ten is rooted in a Catawba word for ten",
    "Comparative Siouan Dictionary includes Woccon language data",
    "Woccon language data comes from historical sources and linguistic reconstruction",
    "Catawba Saraw dialect provides comparative data for Woccon words",
    "Woccon bears striking resemblance to Catawba linguistic structures",
    "Woccon data shows similarities to Catawba in modal suffixes and participial constructions",
    "Woccon linguistic analysis is based on comparison with related Catawba language patterns",
    "Woccon is related to Catawba language",
    "Yupyupseunne indicates a river heavily overgrown with trees",
    "Coosauk probably means literally 'Corn-Weed'",
    "into probably literally means clay",
    "waurraupa is a metaphoric extension from white hot",
    "Woccon vocabulary shows systematic relationships to Catawban words",
    "Woccon shows correspondence of wa or ma- with hu- in Proto-North-Caddoan",
    "The name Waccamaw could be derived from waką́n ma 'place of power, sacred place'",
    "Woccon word 'wintoshore' for Englishman may derive from the surname Windsor",
    "Woccon uses 'mau' as postposition meaning 'there', corresponding to Catawba 'ma'",
    "Woccon language data comes from multiple scholarly sources including Taukchiray (2002) and Rudes",
    "John Lawson documented a Woccon vocabulary in 1709",
    "Woccon is studied in connection with Catawba language research",
    "The language has been subject to place name identification studies in the sixteenth-century Southeast",
    "Modern revitalization efforts involve reconciling ethics, Indigenous epistemologies, and colonial data archives",
    "Research is presented at Siouan and Caddoan Languages Conferences",
    "Historical documentation spans from early 1700s through contemporary linguistic research",
    "Woccon was most closely related to Catawba within the Siouan linguistic family",
    "Siouan linguistic family has two branches: Eastern Siouan (Catawba and Woccon) and Western Siouan (Dakota, Ofo, Tutelo, Biloxi, etc.)",
    "Waccamaw territory was from Winyah Bay up Waccamaw River to the Cape Fear, and somewhat to the Neuse River",
    "Catawba and Woccon were structured more similarly to each other, supporting the hypothesis that they were in use early in Carolina prehistory",
    "Woccon is a dormant Eastern Siouan language",
    "The Woccon language closely resembled Catawba, and was considered one of two Catawban languages",
    "Waccon was a subdivision of the Waccamaw"
  ]
